  <ontology>
    <term_definitions language="en">
      <items code="at0000">
        <items id="text">Exclusion statement</items>
        <items id="description">Statement about clinical conditions, issues or concerns that are either not currently present and/or have not been present in the past.</items>
      </items>
      <items code="at0001">
        <items id="text">Tree</items>
        <items id="description">@ internal @</items>
      </items>
      <items code="at0002">
        <items id="text">Exclusion statement</items>
        <items id="description">Statement of exclusion of a condition, issue or concern.</items>
        <items id="comment">Use to capture any health information that needs to be explicitly recorded in the health record as not currently present and/or not present in the past. This statement can support recording general statements  such as "No previous/past ..." or " No known ...". Or it can support more exacting statements about a specified object such as "No known history of ..." where the 'Specific Item' identifies the precise condition, issue or concern. See specialisations for Medications, Adverse Reactions, Family History, Problem/Diagnosis and Procedures as common examples that have been modelled explicitly for common use.</items>
      </items>
      <items code="at0003">
        <items id="text">Specific item</items>
        <items id="description">Identification of the specific clinical item to which the 'Exclusion statement' applies.</items>
        <items id="comment">In this generic context of this parent archetype it is not easy to identify a commonly used example, however in context of the specialisations from this archetype, this data element would be used to enable exact statements that include: corticosteriods have never been used; there has never been an adverse reaction to penicillin; there is no family history of heart disease; or there is no past history of diabetes for the individual. In these examples, 'corticosteroids', 'penicillin', 'heart disease', 'diabetes' would be examples of the 'Specific item' and the rest of the example phrase would be carried in the 'Exclusion statement'. Other possible use cases might be: exclusion of pregnancy, never X-rayed, or received a blood transfusion. Please note: if precoordinated phrases such as  'No known allergy to penicillin' is available, then this can be used as an alternative in the 'Exclusion statement' alone.</items>
      </items>
      <items code="at0004">
        <items id="text">Last updated</items>
        <items id="description">The date on which this exclusion was last updated.</items>
      </items>
      <items code="at0006">
        <items id="text">Tree</items>
        <items id="description">@ internal @</items>
      </items>
      <items code="at0007">
        <items id="text">Comment</items>
        <items id="description">Additional narrative about the Exclusion not captured in other fields.</items>
      </items>
      <items code="at0008">
        <items id="text">Extension</items>
        <items id="description">Additional information required to capture local content or to align with other reference models/formalisms.</items>
        <items id="comment">For example: local information requirements or additional metadata to align with FHIR or CIMI equivalents.</items>
      </items>
    </term_definitions>
  </ontology>
